<description>
Translate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.

Input structure
A grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic
4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line
sprites.

Rule
Preserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and
place the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular
frame exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.

Key insight
The output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count
determines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame
assembly with the least difference between height and width.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_to_match",
  "src": "monochromatic 4-connected line sprite s",
  "dst": "frame-border slot d in a complete one-cell-thick rectangular frame",
  "match_cond": "s.shape and s.orientation fit d without rotation; assigned slots cover every border pixel exactly once",
  "transform": "translate s into d; fill the frame interior with the background color",
  "grid_shape": "most balanced rectangle whose border pixel count equals the total foreground pixel count"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task_type: move_to_match
  grid:
    input: 6-16 rows x 6-16 cols; single dominant background color fills all non-sprite cells
    output: most-balanced rectangle whose border perimeter equals total foreground pixel count; 4-8 rows/cols
  sprites:
    count: 4-5 monochromatic 4-connected objects, each a distinct non-bg color
    shapes: mix of straight bars (including single pixel) and L-corners; bars have bbox 1xN or Nx1 (area 1-7); L-corners have exactly one 90-degree bend with area = h+w-1 (3-7), bbox 2-6 x 2-5
    input_placement: scattered on bg, non-overlapping; different-colored sprites may be 4-adjacent; no rotation
  output_frame:
    structure: 1-cell-thick rectangular frame; every border cell is foreground, interior is bg
    tiling: sprites translated (no rotation/flip) to cover border exactly once; each sprite occupies a contiguous arc of the perimeter
    L_corner_rule: each L-corner sprite's elbow aligns with a frame corner it covers
    uniqueness: the most-balanced (min |h-w|) valid tiling must be unique
  diagram: |
    A A A B      each letter = one sprite color
    D . . B      dots = bg interior
    D . . B
    D C C C
generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first, then invert
  steps:
    - Pick bg color and 4-5 distinct foreground colors. Choose frame size h x w (4-8, prefer near-square) giving perimeter P = 2h+2w-4 in range 12-28.
    - Partition the frame border clockwise into 4-5 contiguous arcs (2-3 L-corner arcs spanning exactly one frame corner each, plus 1-2 straight-bar arcs on sides). Vary arc lengths (1-7) asymmetrically to ensure unique tiling.
    - Build the output grid (frame with bg interior). Extract each arc as a relative sprite shape.
    - Verify uniqueness by checking no other arc partition of the same frame reproduces the same set of sprite shapes. Reject and re-partition if ambiguous.
    - Place sprites on a larger input grid (>= frame size + padding) at random non-overlapping positions, preserving shape/orientation exactly.
